## Green Chemistry
Abstract
Aerobic oxidation of carbohydrates-derived 5-hydromethylfuran (HMF) into high added-value 2,5-diformylfuran (DFF) has attracted much attention in recent years. However, the direct synthesis of DFF from cheap and abundant carbohydrates via HMF as intermediate through a one-pot process is highly desirable but challenging. In this work, we have developed a highly efficient and recyclable non-noble heterogeneous catalyst for one-pot conversion of fructose into DFF with extremely high yields $(>99\%)$ . The catalyst was prepared by a simple pyrolysis method using Fe-based metal-organic frameworks (MOF) as template and sulfur powder as dopant. The pyrolysis of MOF template under interactions of Ostwald ripening and Kirkendall effects led to the formation of uniform octahedral $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles with exposed (111) crystal facets highly dispersed on sulfur doped carbon. The superior selectivity to DFF over the designed Fe-based catalyst is related to the low adsorption energy of DFF on the support as well as the existence of non-oxidized sulfur that makes the catalytic system less oxidative.
## 1. Introduction
Faced with the increasing energy consumption and fossil energy exhaustion, scientists have been focusing on alternative energies in recent decades.1-3 Compared to crude oil, biomass emerged as an unique carbon reservoir for the production of biofuels, chemicals and sustainable energy.4-7 The complexity of biomass is exemplified in the presence of certain fractions (cellulose, lignin) from which a number of functional compounds denoted as platform chemicals can be derived.2,4 With the investigation of novel catalysts and reaction routes, key platform molecules including 5-hydroxymethylfurfural (HMF) and furfural (FFA) can be of high interest in the production of high added-value chemicals.4,8-10
HMF is a representative biomass platform molecule derived from carbohydrates, which could also be converted into important compounds such as 2,5-diformylfuran (DFF). DFF has a high significance in pharmaceuticals, fungicides and functional polymers production. However, the reported routes for DFF production are mostly based on aerobic oxidation of pure HMF.11-15 From the viewpoint of atom economy and scale-up production, bio-industry still calls for robust routes transferring original biomass directly into final products simultaneously satisfying energy, economic and environmental requirements.16-18 The direct synthesis of DFF from cheap and abundant carbohydrates via HMF as intermediate through a one-pot process is highly desirable.19-21
Acidic sites as well as inert atmosphere have been reported to be favorable for sugars dehydration, while molecular oxygen is necessary for HMF oxidation.22-27 In
## Green Chemistry
this regard, Takagaki et al. developed a novel route using acidic ABS-15 and Ru/HT as catalyst for one-pot fructose and glucose conversion, obtaining $49\%$ and $25\%$ DFF yields from fructose and glucose, respectively. Fu and co-workers took advantage of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -SBA- $.\mathrm{{SO}_{3}H}$ and $\mathrm{KMn}_{8}\mathrm{O}_{16}{\cdot}\mathrm{nH}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in catalyzing fructose conversion into DFF and achieved a yield as high as $80\%$ .29 However, those reaction systems suffered from undesired side reactions and a large formation of byproducts (e.g., FFCA and FDCA). On the other hand, the stepwise addition of catalysts complicates a potential implementation for industrial production. Up to date, the development of facile and highly selective one-pot strategies for fructose conversion into DFF is still challenging.
Herein, we report a novel non-noble heterogeneous catalyst for one-pot conversion of fructose into DFF. The catalyst was simply synthesized through pyrolysis by using a Fe-containing metal-organic framework (MIL-88B) as sacrificial template and S powder as dopant. The obtained Fe-based catalyst possessed small quantities of acidic sulfur-derived functional groups with highly uniform octahedral nanoparticles featuring $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (111) exposed crystal faces. The proposed Fe system exhibited quantitative yields to DFF from fructose. The superior selectivity to DFF resulted from interactions of the low adsorption energy of DFF on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (111) crystal faces and the low oxidation ability of non-oxidized S element doped in the amorphous carbon matrix. In addition, the magnetic catalyst could be easily separated after reaction and reused over 6 runs without any significant reactivity loss.
## 2. Experimental
All reagents were of analytical grade and were used without further purification.
## 2.1. Synthesis of MIL-88B
In a typical synthesis, $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left(1.62\ \mathrm{g}\right)$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ (0.996 g) were dissolved in DMF $(30~\mathrm{mL})$ ) at room temperature to form a clear solution. The mixture was sealed in a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ autoclave to crystalline at $373\mathrm{~K~}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . The resulting brown powder was washed with DMF several times and then dried under vacuum at $323\mathrm{~K~}$ overnight.
## 2.2. Synthesis of Fe/C-S materials
Fe/C-S materials were prepared as follow: a mixture of MIL-88B (Fe) (0.5 g)
## Green Chemistry
and S powder $(1.0\ \mathrm{g})$ was heated at a certain temperature for $5\mathrm{h}$ with a heating rate of $1~{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}~\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ from room temperature in argon atmosphere. After the heating treatment, the resultant sample was treated at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ under vacuum. The as-prepared material was denoted as $\mathrm{Fe/C{-}S}$ (ca. 0.3 g).
The Fe/C-S catalyst was dispersed in aqua regia for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ to completely remove Fe. The obtained solid was washed with ethanol and collected by centrifugation, and then dried at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ under vacuum. The as-synthesized material was denoted as $\mathrm{Fe/C-S(H^{+})}$ .
## 2.3. Catalysts characterization
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the samples were obtained on a Rigaku diffractometer (D/MAX-IIIA, 3kW) using Cu Kα radiation $(40\mathrm{kV},30\mathrm{mA},i$ $\lambda$ $=0.1543\ \mathrm{nm}$ ). BET surface areas and pore size measurements were performed with $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ on a Micromeritics ASAP 2020M instrument. Before measurements, samples were degassed at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . The iron contents in the samples were measured quantitatively by atomic absorption spectroscopy (AAS) on a HITACHI Z-2300 instrument. Elemental analysis was performed on an Elementar Vario EL III equipment by weighing samples of 0.2–0.3 mg and packing with aluminium foil for the measurement. Raman spectra were recorded on a LabRAM Aramis Raman Spectrometer (HORIBA Jobin Yvon). The surface acidity was measured in a dynamic mode by means of a pulse chromatographic technique of gas phase $(200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ adsorption of 2,6-dimethylpyridine (DMPY, Brönsted sites) as probe molecule. The magnetic properties of the catalyst were measured on a Quantum Design physical property measurement system (PPMS-9).
The size and morphology of materials were studied by high-resolution scanning electron microscopy (HR-SEM). Transmission electron micrographs (TEM) and high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) were recorded on a JEOL JEM-2010F instrument equipped with EDX analysis (Bruker XFlash 5030T) operated at $200\mathrm{kV}.$ Samples were suspended in ethanol and deposited straightaway on a copper grid prior to analysis.
ATR-IR measurements were carried out on a Thermo Fisher iS10 equipped with a liquid nitrogen cooled MCT detector. The spectra were obtained by averaging 32 scans at the resolution of $1~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . The thin film of catalyst powder deposited on the ZnSe element for ATR-IR spectroscopic study was prepared as follow. A suspension of ca. $50~\mathrm{mg}$ catalyst powder and 0.1 mmol of HMF (or DFF) in $2~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol was stirred overnight to eliminate any agglomeration, after which, the slurry was dropped onto the ZnSe internal reflection element (IRE) for spectra recording. The IR spectrum of pure catalyst in solvent was acquired as background.
XPS measurements were performed in a ultra-high vacuum (UHV) multipurpose surface analysis system (SpecsTM model Germany) operating at pressures $<10^{-10}$ mbar using a conventional X-ray source (XR-50, Specs, Mg Kalpha, 1253.6 eV) in a “stop-and-go” mode to reduce potential damage due to sample irradiation. Detailed Fe high-resolution spectra (pass energy 25 and $10\mathrm{eV},$ step size 1 and $0.1\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively)
## Green Chemistry
were recorded with a Phoibos 150-MCD energy analyzer. Binding energies were referenced to the C1s line at 284.6 eV from adventitious carbon. Deconvolution curves for the XPS spectra were obtained using software supplied by the spectrometer manufacturer.
## 2.4. General procedures for one-pot fructose conversion
The one-pot fructose conversion was carried out in a stainless steel reactor. Fructose $\cdot1.2\ \mathrm{mmol}_{,}^{\cdot}$ ), catalyst (metal $20\mathrm{mol}\%$ ) and ethanol $2~\mathrm{mL}$ were added into the reactor. The sealed reactor was purged several times with nitrogen to remove air then maintained at $3\ \mathrm{MPa}$ at room temperature. After that, the reactor was heated up to $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a $900\ \mathrm{rpm}$ stirring speed. During the reaction, samples were taken and analyzed by HPLC (Agilent 1260), and the first sample was taken when the reaction temperature reached $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . For the recyclability test, the catalyst was separated by a magnet, washed by ethanol and then reused directly.
The conversion of fructose $(\mathrm{mol\%})$ ), HMF selectivity $(\mathrm{mol\%})$ and yield of products were calculated as follows:
