## Results and discussion
Scheme 1 shows the typical synthesis route of $\mathrm{Fe/C-S}$ materials. Firstly, MIL-88B (MIL: Matériaux de I’Institut Lavoisier) was prepared according to the literature report by a hydrothermal method.30 After the addition of a certain amount of dopant S powder and thorough grinding, the mixture was transferred into a quartz boat and thermally treated at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{~h~}$ under Ar atmosphere. The pyrolysis temperature was selected based on the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) of MIL-88B which showed the structure collapses at ca. $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .30 The obtained black powder was denoted as $\mathrm{Fe/C{-}S}$ . For comparison, $\mathrm{Fe/C}$ without S doping was synthesized by using the same strategy. Fe, C and S contents were determined by AAS and elemental analysis and the results are summarized in Table 1. Compared with $\mathrm{Fe/C}$ , the Fe content almost remained unchanged after doping with sulfur (ca. $8\mathrm{\mt{\%}}$ ), while C content slightly decreased.
The surface areas as well as porosities of $\mathrm{Fe/C{-}S}$ materials were characterized by $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption/desorption measurements (Fig. 1a). Compared with Fe/C, BET surface
## Green Chemistry
areas of Fe/C-S remained unchanged. In addition, the MOF-derived materials clearly showed both microporous and mesoporous structures. No obvious differences could be observed after S doping, indicating that S doping did not significantly influence surface areas and porosity.
PXRD patterns of the materials were shown in Fig. 1b. Characteristic peaks at $30.2^{\mathrm{o}}$ and $35.6^{\circ}$ indicated the formation of a $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ phase (JCPDS No.65-3107) through pyrolysis at a high temperature. For $\mathrm{Fe/C{-}S}$ , there was a significant wide peak from $10^{\circ}$ to $20^{\circ}$ , indicating the existence of amorphous carbon derived from organic ligands of MIL-88B. No other significant difference could be observed between Fe/C and Fe/C-S patterns, confirming the structure was mostly preserved after sulfonation.
Raman spectroscopy was also carried out for the materials and the results have been summarized in Fig. 1c. Two peaks at 1280 and $1568~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were assigned to the D band and G band, respectively. Defects and distortion of carbon layers were related to the D band, oppositely, crystalline and graphitic structure of carbon layers could be analyzed through the G band.
