Following characterization, Fe-based materials were employed as catalysts in the one-pot fructose conversion to DFF. Table 2 shows reaction results using different catalysts. Fe-based catalysts exhibited a high reactivity in fructose dehydration under $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere while $\mathrm{Fe/C{-}S}$ gave a better selectivity to DFF without any byproduct in a subsequent HMF oxidation (entries 1 and 3). With the increment of catalyst loading, fructose conversion was significantly enhanced at extremely high DFF selectivities (entries 2-4). Results reported in this work are remarkably improved to those previously reported (Table 3), with the activity of Fe/C-S catalyst being comparable or even higher to that of noble-metal catalysts under environmentally friendly reaction conditions.
Further studies confirmed the high reactivity and selectivity of Fe/C-S in
## Green Chemistry
dehydration. Under optimized reaction conditions, fructose was quickly dehydrated into HMF within 2 hours ( $5\%$ HMF yield, without changing the atmosphere, entry 5), significantly improved as compared to dehydration activity obtained for Fe/C (entry 6).
It should be noted that the reaction could also proceed well at a low catalyst loading (e.g., $5\mathrm{\mol\%}$ ) by slightly altering the reaction conditions, giving $99\%$ fructose conversion with $99\%$ DFF yield within $^{14\mathrm{~h~}}$ (entry 7). Furthermore, to examine the role of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ for the oxidation reaction, we tested the activity of $\mathrm{Fe/C-S(H^{+})}$ that was prepared by immersing the $\mathrm{Fe/C{-}S}$ catalyst in aqua regia to completely remove the Fe species. $\mathrm{Fe/C-S(H^{+})}$ was not active for HMF oxidation, indicating that Fe oxides were essential for the oxidation process.
Details of fructose conversion using $\mathrm{Fe/C{-}S}$ under optimized reaction conditions were further investigated. The yield distribution of the one-pot reaction at different times is depicted in Figure 5. Fructose was initially quantitatively dehydrated to HMF, with yields reaching $599\%$ within 2 hours. A switch to molecular oxygen resulted in a significant decrease in HMF yield with an obvious DFF production. HMF was rapidly oxidized with time of reaction to reach quantitative yields to DFF in less than $3\mathrm{h}$ .
## Green Chemistry
To further interpret the high activity of the $\mathrm{Fe/C{-}S}$ catalyst, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy measurements were carried out to identify the different species in the catalyst (Fig. 6). In the C 1s spectrum, peaks at 284.5, 284.8 and $285.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ could be assigned to $C{=}C$ , C-H and C-C bonds derived from organic ligands of MIL-88B, respectively.41 Two characteristic peaks at 707.8 and $710.6~\mathrm{eV}$ in the Fe $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ region indicated the existence of both $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ species in the catalyst.42 In the S 2p region, the S $2{\mathsf{p}}_{1/2}$ peak at $161.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ could be attributed to S-S bonds, while the strong S $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ signal at $163.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ could be assigned to active S species with S-C bonds in the material. Weak signals at 167.9 and $168.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ could be attributed to trace amounts of oxidized sulfur species, in good agreement with previous literature reports.39
In situ ATR-IR experiments for HMF and DFF adsorption were carried out to simulate reaction conditions and figure out the adsorption of the furanic derivatives. In Fig. 7a, the strong HMF characteristic bands at $1180~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , $1518~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and 1677
## Green Chemistry
$\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (corresponding to C-O-C bonds in furan rings, $C{=}C$ bonds in furan rings and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ stretching bonds) could be visualized. The intensity of these bands was significantly higher on Fe/C-S, indicating a stronger HMF adsorption on Fe/C-S catalysts.43,44 However, there was no significant intensity change in Fig. 7b, resulting from a weak adsorption of DFF on both Fe-based catalysts, being potentially the reason of the high DFF selectivity obtained in the systems and the unexisting generation of by-products.
Furthermore, DFT calculations conducted to further support these findings indicated that he oxygen atoms located in the (111) facet are negatively charged with -0.43 e by Mulliken population (or -0.16 e by Hirshfeld population). As shown in Fig. 8, the negatively charged oxygen species were favorable for HMF oxidation. Nevertheless, the two formyl groups in the DFF molecule strengthen the electrostatic repulsive interaction between the catalyst surface and the DFF molecule, indicating that DFF can be easily desorbed from the catalyst (not strongly adsorbed on the surface), suppressing over-oxidation to side products.
Based on the characterizations and reaction results above, the unprecedented reactivity of $\mathrm{Fe/C{-}S}$ catalyst could be related to different element states of sulfur functional groups providing the optimum dehydration activity in the systems and well distributed iron oxides derived from MOFs with exposed (111) crystal facets. In the catalytic system, fructose was first dehydrated into HMF catalyzed by oxidized sulfur functional groups under nitrogen atmosphere. The generated HMF was strongly adsorbed on the catalyst, oxidized by iron oxides following the Mars-van Krevelen mechanism: one HMF molecule was adsorbed and oxidized by lattice oxygen of iron oxides subsequently, the absent lattice oxygen was immediately filled by molecular oxygen.12 Compared to undoped Fe/C catalyst, Fe/C-S exhibited an extremely high DFF selectivity with the existence of large amounts of non-oxidized S in the carbon layers. Non-oxidized S are believed to make the catalytic system less oxidative,
## Green Chemistry
suppressing side reactions and the generation of over-oxidation products (e.g., FFCA, and FDCA). Additionally, the observed weak adsorption of DFF on the catalysts is believed to contribute to no by-products formation.
Reaction conditions: fructose $1.2\ \mathrm{mmol}_{,}^{\cdot}$ ), catalyst (metal $20\mathrm{\mol\%}$ ), ethanol $2~\mathrm{mL}$ , $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $\Nu_{2}$ (1 bar) for $2\mathrm{h}$ and then changed to $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (1 bar) for $2\mathrm{h}$ .
Fe/C-S exhibited good magnetic properties (Figure 10), with the possibility to be magnetically separated from the reaction mixture after reaction. After magnetic separation from the reaction system, the Fe/C-S catalyst was washed several times with ethanol and dried under vacuum. The recyclability experiments of the $\mathrm{Fe/C{-}S}$ catalyst were conducted under optimized conditions. After six runs, no significant loss in activity and selectivity could be observed (Fig. 9). In addition, leaching experiments were carried out after $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ of reaction (Fig. 9). The liquid phase was filtered off to quantify any potential leached Fe species using AAS. To our delight, no metal leaching could be quantified in the filtrate $(<0.5\mathrm{ppm})$ ), which also exhibited no reactivity in the selected process under optimized reaction conditions, confirming the truly heterogeneous nature of the reaction as well as the high stability of the Fe-C-S system in the selective conversion of fructose to DFF.
## Conclusions
Uniform S doping Fe-based catalysts were successfully synthesized through a facile pyrolysis method using MIL-88B as sacrificial template and S powder as dopant. The obtained octahedral Fe nanoparticles on sulfur-doped carbon exhibited an excelling reactivity in the one-pot fructose conversion with complete fructose conversion and $599\%$ DFF selectivity under mild conditions. We found that fully exposed $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (111) crystal facets were in favor of HMF oxidation and non-oxidized elemental sulfur present in the carbonaceous matrix made the system less oxidative, resulting in a superior selectivity to DFF. In addition, a strong HMF adsorption and
## Green Chemistry
weak DFF adsorption on Fe/C-S also enhanced HMF conversion and suppressed the generation of by-products. This work represents a novel synthetic route to prepare uniform metal nanoparticles with specific crystal surface exposed derived from MOFs and provides new insights into the selective transformation of biomass using recyclable heterogeneous catalysts.
